---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

// Literal reference syntax, kept out of Astro's expression parser.
const cfgRef = '{{cfg:<key>}}';
const lvrRef = '{{lvr:<key>}}';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Private values and local variables — Kvendra developer docs"
  description="Keep identifying values out of knowledge-base text: project-wide private values referenced as cfg references and resolved only to operate, and per-machine local variables substituted only by the local broker."
>
    <span class="kvd-kicker">Reference</span>
    <h1>Private values and local variables</h1>
    <p>
      Knowledge-base text is read by every agent and every member of the
      workspace, and it can be exported or published. Identifying values do not
      belong in it. Kvendra gives you two kinds of reference instead of the
      literal value:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        a <strong>private value</strong>, <code>{cfgRef}</code> — a fact of the
        <em>project</em>, stored once in the knowledge base;
      </li>
      <li>
        a <strong>local variable</strong>, <code>{lvrRef}</code> — a fact of
        <em>one user on one machine</em>, never stored in the knowledge base.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Keys follow <code>[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{'{0,127}'}</code>.
      <strong>Credentials are neither</strong>: they go into the
      <a href="/docs/cli/">CLI vault</a>, never into a knowledge-base entry.
    </p>
    <p class="kvd-callout">
      Both are features of the hosted service. Local variables also need the
      <a href="/docs/cli/">CLI</a> (0.7.0 or later) on each machine.
    </p>

    <h2>Private values</h2>
    <p>
      Use them for identifiers that must not appear in text — account ids,
      local paths, profile ids, person names, case numbers. Each is stored
      <strong>once</strong> as a <code>CFG</code> private value; everything else
      refers to it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Write references, never literal values.</strong> Enter the value
        once, ideally from the dashboard editor so it never passes through a
        language model.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reads never resolve.</strong> <code>entity_get</code>,
        <code>entity_query</code>, <code>entity_search</code>,
        <code>entity_related</code> and the export return references raw and
        list them in <code>private_refs</code>. Edit the text and write it back
        as-is.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resolve only to operate</strong>, with
        <code>config_get</code> or:
      </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>private_value_resolve({'{'} keys: ["&lt;key&gt;", ...], project_id {'}'})   // 1–50 exact keys</code></pre>
    <p>
      Resolution needs the <code>cfg:resolve</code> permission, is audited per
      request (keys and counts, never values) and is served with
      <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>. Use the value for the operation and
      never paste it back into text.
    </p>
    <p>
      Writes are checked. Text carrying an unescaped display marker is rejected
      with <code>400 private_marker_in_text</code>; to mention the syntax
      literally, escape it with a leading backslash. Two warnings help you keep
      text clean: <code>private_value_suggested</code> (this looks like a value
      that should be a reference) and <code>private_ref_undefined</code> (the
      key you referenced does not exist).
    </p>

    <h2>Local variables</h2>
    <p>
      Use them for what differs per machine: your workspace path, the AWS
      profile of this laptop, a local host or port.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Declare</strong> the variable in the knowledge base as a
        <code>CFG</code> with
        <code>metadata: {'{'} kind: "local_var", key, type, description {'}'}</code>
        and <strong>no value</strong>. <code>type</code> is one of
        <code>path</code>, <code>host</code>, <code>port</code>,
        <code>profile_id</code>, <code>string</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Set</strong> the value on each machine, by a human in a real
        terminal: <code>kvendra vars set &lt;key&gt; --type &lt;type&gt;</code>.
        It lives only in that machine's local vault.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reference</strong> it as <code>{lvrRef}</code> only where the
        broker consumes it — a working directory, a profile id, a bounded
        argument, a URL. The local broker substitutes it inside its primitive,
        validates it by type and masks it in its output.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The engine never has the value. Referencing a key with no declaration
      returns the warning <code>local_var_undeclared</code>. When content is
      published, each local reference is replaced by a placeholder.
    </p>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .kvd-callout {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--phos);
    padding-left: var(--sp-4);
    color: var(--fog);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  }
</style>
